  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "Tell nearly always has a person right after it (tell me, tell your sister), and the thing told comes after the person. The exceptions are a few fixed objects: tell the truth, tell a lie, tell a story, tell a joke, tell the time, tell the difference. To report someone's exact words, use say: \"No,\" she said.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "He told that he was busy.",
      "correct": "He told me that he was busy.",
      "note": "Tell needs a person after it; without a person, use say: He said that he was busy."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "She told to me the answer.",
      "correct": "She told me the answer.",
      "note": "The person comes right after tell, with no to."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "Tell me where is the station.",
      "correct": "Tell me where the station is.",
      "note": "A reported question after tell keeps the word order of a statement."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Tell covers giving information, telling stories, giving orders, and knowing something from signs (I can tell). Many languages use separate verbs for these; the knowing use in particular is rarely the same verb as the speech verb.",
    "grammar": "The person is the first object: tell me the answer, tell her that, tell him to go. When the words come first, English uses say and the person takes to. A reported question after tell keeps statement word order: tell me where it is.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The past tense and past participle told have the vowel of cold and do not rhyme with tell."
  },
